Under New Zealand's Abortion Legislation Act 2020, abortions are legally permitted through medical or surgical procedures depending on gestational age, and can be performed up to birth if a health practitioner deems it clinically appropriate and consults with another practitioner, as detailed by [Family Life International NZ](https://fli.org.nz/life-faith-family-resources/abortion/nz-abortion-law/) and [Women's Health NZ](https://www.womens-health.org.nz/health-topics/abortion-termination-of-pregnancy/). While the claim author, [Right To Life UK](https://righttolife.org.uk/news/nz-pm-rushes-worlds-most-extreme-abortion-law-into-law-while-country-distracted-with-pandemic), asserts that the law permits controversial methods such as "intact dilation and extraction" (partial-birth abortion) due to a lack of specific statutory bans, there is a lack of independent, objective evidence to verify whether this specific technique is legally permitted or practiced. Because the independent sources do not clarify whether "later surgical abortion" includes intact dilation and extraction, there is not enough information to definitively verify the claim.